---
scope: src/tools/validate
intent: >
  aide_validate is the health check — the tool an agent calls to find
  out whether a project's spec layout is internally consistent. It
  surfaces the classes of damage that accumulate as specs drift away
  from the code they govern: naming conflicts, orphaned research,
  missing specs on orchestrators that clearly deserve one, and broken
  relative links inside spec bodies. Its job is to give the agent a
  findings list it can fix with scaffold and read, not to produce a
  compliance report.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - A call with no path scans the entire project; a call with a path
      scans only that subtree. Scoped runs are first-class because QA
      and fix agents work one module at a time.
    - Findings are reported one per line with a bracketed issue tag
      (`[naming-conflict]`, `[orphaned-research]`, `[missing-spec]`,
      `[orphaned-spec]`, `[broken-link]`), the offending path, and a
      human-readable explanation. The tag lets the agent pattern-match
      on severity and kind; the path lets it act.
    - Deterministic findings — naming conflicts, orphaned research,
      broken links — are labeled as errors. Heuristic findings —
      missing specs on import-heavy orchestrators, orphaned specs on
      folders with no index.ts — are labeled as warnings, because the
      heuristic can be wrong.
    - A clean run returns "No issues found." and nothing else. Silence
      is a valid, expected response, not an error state to work around.
    - Broken-link detection resolves relative paths inside spec bodies
      against the spec's own directory. A link that does not resolve
      is a finding; a link that is a wikilink or a URL is not this
      tool's concern.
  undesired:
    - A run that throws on an unreadable file and aborts the whole
      scan. One corrupt spec should not blind the agent to every other
      finding in the project. Unreadable files are skipped, silently
      on the happy path and with a warning otherwise.
    - A run that labels every finding as an error regardless of how
      the finding was reached. Heuristic findings have false positives
      by design; calling them errors makes the agent treat them with
      certainty it should not have.
    - A run whose output dumps a JSON envelope with `warnings` and
      `errors` arrays. The agent is reading findings; it should not
      need a parser to do so.
    - A run that invents findings for folders the user did not ask
      about. Scoped runs respect their scope, because the whole point
      of scoping is keeping output focused on the module under
      review.
    - A run that treats "no findings" as a failure condition — empty
      output, an error, or a warning about "nothing to validate". A
      clean project is the goal state.
---

## Context

Spec layouts drift. A research spec gets added next to an existing
`.aide` without the rename. A folder gets deleted but its sibling
research spec does not. A link in a spec body points at a helper that
has since been renamed. None of these are programming bugs, but each
one erodes the contract between specs and the code they govern. This
tool is the one place that drift is detected and named, so agents can
fix it before the spec layout stops being trustworthy.

This tool is **not** the audit agent from the automated QA loop.
`aide_validate` detects drift in the spec *layout* — files in the
wrong place, names that violate the naming rule, relative links that
do not resolve. The QA-loop audit agent detects drift in the
*generated output* against a spec's `outcomes.desired` and
`outcomes.undesired`, and it runs as a slash-command agent session
scaffolded by `aide_init`, not as an MCP tool. Both checks are useful;
they operate on different artifacts and live in different layers of
the pipeline. This tool owns the layout layer; the audit agent owns
the output layer.

The tool shares the same scanning primitives as discover — it walks
the file tree, classifies files, and reports — but the two tools read
the same data for different purposes. Discover produces a map; validate
produces findings. Keeping them separate lets each stay honest about
its own job.

## Strategy

**Separate deterministic findings from heuristic ones.** Naming
conflicts, orphaned research, and broken links are facts about the
file system that admit no interpretation: either the folder contains
both `.aide` and `intent.aide` or it does not. Missing specs and
orphaned specs, by contrast, are inferred from import counts and
folder shapes — useful signals, but wrong often enough that an agent
acting on them with certainty will make bad edits. The tool reports
both, but labels the deterministic class as errors and the heuristic
class as warnings. The agent decides how much trust to put in each
class from the label alone.

**Scoped runs are first-class.** When a QA agent has narrowed its
attention to `src/service/notifications`, it calls validate with that
path and expects findings from that subtree only. The alternative —
validating the whole project and expecting the agent to filter —
wastes context and dilutes the signal. Scope is a parameter, not a
filter applied after the fact.

**Resolve relative links against the spec's own directory.** A link
like `../orders/index.ts` in a spec at `src/service/notifications/.aide`
is a pointer the reader can follow. If the target does not exist, the
pointer is broken and the agent needs to know. Wikilinks and URLs are
out of scope for this tool — they belong to a knowledge base and a
network, neither of which validate can reach. The link check is a
local, offline operation, and that is exactly why it is reliable.

**Silence is the success state.** A clean project returns "No issues
found." and nothing else. Agents reading that output reason about it
correctly because it is unambiguous; any other convention (empty
string, "OK", a checkmark) invites interpretation the tool does not
need.

## Good examples

An agent calls `aide_validate` on a project with drift and receives:

> [naming-conflict] src/service/foo — folder contains both `.aide` and `intent.aide`
> [orphaned-research] src/service/bar — `research.aide` has no intent spec alongside
> [broken-link] src/service/baz/.aide — relative link does not resolve: ../old/index.ts
> [missing-spec] src/service/qux — orchestrator imports 4 helpers but has no `.aide`

Four findings, three classes. The first three are errors the agent
can fix mechanically; the fourth is a warning the agent should
investigate before acting on, because "four helpers" is a heuristic
not a rule.

An agent calls `aide_validate` with `path=src/util/configureIde` on a
clean module and receives:

> No issues found.

Scoped, clean, one-line response. The agent moves on.

## Bad examples

A run that crashes on a single unreadable file and returns an MCP
error, leaving the agent with no findings and no way to tell whether
the project was clean or catastrophically broken. One unreadable spec
should not erase the rest of the scan.

A run that emits:

> {"errors":[{"kind":"naming-conflict","path":"src/service/foo"}],"warnings":[]}

The agent is reading a sentence; the tool is returning a schema.
Every token in the envelope is a token the explanation could have
used.

A scoped run on `src/service/notifications` that returns findings
from `src/service/orders` because "they looked related". The caller's
scope was explicit. Widening it silently corrupts the agent's mental
model of what the findings mean.

A clean run that returns an empty string instead of "No issues found."
The agent is left to guess whether the scan produced nothing because
the project is clean or because something went wrong. The guessing is
the bug.
